spatial-inversion.interpretation:
In this equation, 
\mathbf{r}
 represents the position vector of a point in three-dimensional space. It is a vector quantity, meaning it has both magnitude (distance from the origin) and direction. The arrow above 
\mathbf{r}
 denotes that it is a vector. The primed symbol 
\mathbf{r}'
 represents the position vector of the transformed point after the spatial inversion operation. The equation 
\mathbf{r}' = -\mathbf{r}
 signifies that the new position vector 
\mathbf{r}'
 has the same magnitude as the original position vector 
\mathbf{r}
, but its direction is precisely opposite. For instance, if 
\mathbf{r}
 points towards the positive x-axis, 
\mathbf{r}'
 will point towards the negative x-axis with the same length. This transformation is a rigid rotation by 180 degrees about any axis lying in the plane perpendicular to 
\mathbf{r}
 and passing through the origin, or equivalently, a reflection through the origin.

spatial-inversion.symmetry_origin:
Spatial inversion is a fundamental symmetry operation in physics. It is closely related to the concept of parity. Parity symmetry (P) is conserved in some fundamental interactions, such as electromagnetism and gravity, but violated in others, like the weak nuclear force. A physical system or law is said to have parity symmetry if it remains unchanged when all spatial coordinates are inverted (i.e., 
\mathbf{r} → -\mathbf{r}
). Many physical laws, like Newton's law of gravitation and Maxwell's equations, are invariant under spatial inversion, indicating they possess parity symmetry. The origin of this symmetry lies in the geometric properties of space itself and the fundamental forces that govern interactions within it.
